Analyzing the Fields
By running a sample query, you can examine the available attributes and identify which fields matter most for your inventory:

Available Fields: id, name, type, tenantId, kind, location, resourceGroup, subscriptionId, managedBy, sku, plan, properties, tags, identity, zones

Core Identifiers Selected:

name – The friendly name of the resource.

type – The technical classification of the resource.

location – The geographic region where the resource is deployed.

Note on Location Filtering: Because resources span multiple geographic regions and no single region is targeted exclusively, the location field is kept for visibility rather than used as an active search or filter criterion.

Step 2: Mapping Resource Types
Next, analyze the distinct resource types present across the tenant and evaluate their distribution to understand the environment's scope.

A broad scan of the type field reveals a diverse footprint:

1- Virtual Machines (virtualmachines) & Disks (disk)
2- Network Interfaces (networkinterfaces), Virtual Networks (virtualnetworks), and Public IP Addresses (publicipaddresses)
3- Network Security Groups (networksecuritygroups) & Route Tables (routetables)
4- Storage Accounts (storageaccounts)
5- App Service Plans (serverfarms) and Web Apps (sites)
6- Private Endpoints (privateendpoints) & Private DNS Zones (privatednszones)
7- Monitoring & Governance: Data Collection Rules (datacollectionrules), Metric Alerts (metricalerts), Activity Log Alerts (activitylogalerts), Action Groups (actiongroups), and Key Vaults (vaults)
8- Application Insights Components (components), Workbooks (workbooks), and User-Assigned Identities (userassignedidentities)

Step 3: Shortlisting the Top 10 Critical Assets
With dozens of resource types discovered, the next objective is to narrow down the inventory to the top 10 most critical core resources required for foundational tracking, security governance, and operational oversight.

Step 4: Focusing on Virtual Machines (VMs)
When beginning the deep dive into individual asset types, start with Virtual Machines (Microsoft.Compute/virtualmachines) as the primary workload baseline.

Validation & Verification Workflow
Before writing or executing KQL queries, establish an explicit validation methodology to ensure absolute data accuracy across your environment:

- Pre-Query Baseline: Determine the expected number of VMs prior to running the search query.
- Resource Graph Verification: Execute the KQL query to list VMs and obtain their exact count and regional distribution.
- Portal Cross-Check: Manually verify the VM count directly within the Virtual Machines blade in the Azure portal to ensure parity.
- SIEM / Telemetry Check: Cross-reference the discovered VMs against your security monitoring platform (e.g., Microsoft Sentinel) to verify that all instances are actively logging and reporting.

Validation Checklist
[ ] Confirm the total number of VMs returned by the Resource Group KQL query. (done)
[ ] Cross-verify the count manually inside the native Azure VM service. (done)
[ ] Audit telemetry coverage to confirm how many of these VMs are successfully reporting to Sentinel.

To verify whether all virtual machines are actively reporting telemetry to Microsoft Sentinel, you can query the Heartbeat table.

